-- ============================================================================
-- Migration 79: `name_key` — identity, separate from the display name (PC.3).
--
-- THE DEFECT. `upsert_entity` looks up on `(canonical_name, entity_type)`, the
-- UNIQUE index from migration 39, and `entity_persistence_service` sets
-- `canonical_name` to the RAW surface form. So `Brede Welvaart` and
-- `brede welvaart` are two rows — not because cross-document resolution is off,
-- but because the write key is unnormalised. Enabling resolution leaves every
-- pair the resolver misses, and its candidate fetch is capped, so it misses some
-- by construction.
--
-- `canonical_name` stays the DISPLAY form — the curator queue, the exports and
-- the graph all render it. `name_key` carries
-- `shared.utils.name_normalizer.normalize_entity_name(...)` and takes the
-- identity role. PC.2 drew that distinction and named this function as the
-- identity rule, as against `fold_for_comparison` for comparison.
--
-- WHY THIS REFUSES ON A POPULATED TABLE. The value cannot be computed here:
-- `normalize_entity_name` is Python — it strips leading articles, canonicalises
-- documented spelling variants and expands curated org aliases — and a migration
-- body is SurrealQL. Two consequences, both measured against a container rather
-- than assumed:
--
--   * A UNIQUE index treats NONE as a VALUE. Two rows with `name_key = NONE` and
--     the same `entity_type` collide ("Database index already contains
--     [NONE, 'org']"), so adding the column nullable and backfilling afterwards
--     would reject every second row per type. The column must be populated
--     before the index exists.
--   * `THROW` inside `IF { }` does surface: the runner routes bodies through
--     `execute_transaction`, which inspects every statement, so a refusal rolls
--     the migration back and does not bump the version. (The plain
--     `execute_query` seam would have swallowed it — that path returns only the
--     first statement's result.)
--
-- So a populated database is told to run `scripts/backfill_name_key.py` first.
-- That tool computes the keys in Python, and REFUSES when two rows normalise to
-- one key rather than merging them: merging is destructive and is a curator's
-- decision, for which PC.2 shipped the door (`fold_equal`, same type, auto-merge
-- band). A migration that silently merged two entities would be the exact
-- failure PC.6 spent five review rounds making unreachable.
--
-- The old `(canonical_name, entity_type)` index is KEPT. It is strictly weaker
-- than the new one — one display name always maps to one key, while many display
-- names may map to one key — so it costs nothing and keeps existing readers
-- honest.
-- ============================================================================

LET $unkeyed = (SELECT count() FROM entity WHERE name_key = NONE GROUP ALL)[0].count ?? 0;
IF $unkeyed > 0 {
    THROW "migration 79 refuses: " + <string> $unkeyed + " entity row(s) have no "
        + "`name_key`. It cannot be computed in SurrealQL — run "
        + "`uv run python scripts/backfill_name_key.py` first. That tool reports "
        + "any rows that normalise to the same key instead of merging them; "
        + "resolve those through the curator queue, then re-run migrations.";
};

DEFINE FIELD IF NOT EXISTS name_key ON entity TYPE string;
DEFINE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_entity_name_key ON entity FIELDS name_key, entity_type UNIQUE;
